// src/v4/extdeps/formats/yaml.dag
//
// Scope: YAML data format model — direction-agnostic.
// Anchor: https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/ (YAML 1.2.2 spec)
//
// Owns:
//   - YamlValue = Null | Bool | Int | Float | String | Sequence | Mapping (closed sum)
//   - Mapping = Map<YamlValue, YamlValue> (YAML allows non-string keys)
//   - Sequence = List<YamlValue>
//   - Anchor/Alias resolution (YAML's structure-sharing — must resolve before producing typed value)
//   - parse(String) -> Result<YamlValue, Diagnostic>
//   - emit(YamlValue) -> String
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/diagnostic.dag, std/collection.dag
//
// Status: scaffold — fill per TASKS.md T-4.6
// Brief: see BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md; this file's I/O contract is the immutable
// contract for the worker. Splitting this file requires explicit operator
// ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).

module v4.extdeps.formats.yaml


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CANONICAL-MIRROR (T-4.6 fan-out). This file EXACT-mirrors the ratified
// canonical src/v4/extdeps/formats/json.dag seam (PR #3184, T-4 mgr
// seam-review ratification msg_f5971a8a): the format's closed recursive
// value sum + node.dag A1 name-reference recursion + the Practice-4
// five-pattern ledger; the parse/emit carrier seam = `Outcome<YamlValue>`
// (the ONE operator-ratified D1 carrier, std/diagnostic.dag DECISIONS
// item **I** — NOT a per-file sum); operations deferred-with-named-owner.
// Same discipline, YAML-anchored facts.
//
// DECLARATIVE-NOW / OPERATIONS-DEFERRED-WITH-OWNER. Modeled NOW: the
// `YamlValue` data model — declaration-class, substrate-faithful. DEFERRED
// (🟡, named owner + dissolution trigger, NEVER improvised, NEVER a
// bodiless `fn` — the frozen v2 seed rejects bodiless `fn`, DECISIONS
// PARSE-1): the parse/emit bodies (incl. YAML anchor/alias + tag
// resolution) and the numeric interpretation of int/float lexemes. The
// operator-sanctioned pattern verified on std/collection.dag #3169 and
// the canonical json.dag.
//
// CARRIER SEAM = Outcome<YamlValue> (NOT a per-file sum). The header's
// `parse(String) -> Result<YamlValue, Diagnostic>` prose denotes the
// ratified D1 carrier `Outcome<T> = Produced { value: T } | Rejected
// { diagnostic: Diagnostic }` (std/diagnostic.dag, DECISIONS item **I**;
// `Result<…, Diagnostic>` prose names this same surface — no parallel
// `Result<ok, err>` in std/). When the parse op is authored (trigger
// below) it returns `Outcome<YamlValue>` — the SAME carrier all six
// T-4.6 formats consume.
//
// FAIL-CLOSED, NO FABRICATION (YAML 1.2.2; brief §C binding STOP). Every
// non-conforming input is `Rejected { diagnostic }` — never a partial
// parse, never a defaulted/guessed YamlValue. Two YAML-specific
// structural guarantees, both the json.dag dup-key discipline applied to
// YAML:
//   - ANCHOR/ALIAS (YAML 1.2.2 §3.2.2 / §7.1): `&anchor`/`*alias` is
//     structure-SHARING, resolved DURING parse — a produced `YamlValue`
//     is alias-free BY CONSTRUCTION (the data model has no alias node;
//     aliases are resolved against their anchors while building the
//     typed value, exactly as json's `Map` makes a parsed object
//     dup-free by construction). An UNRESOLVABLE alias (no matching
//     anchor) or an alias CYCLE is a fail-closed parse `Rejected`
//     (a cyclic YAML graph is not a finite `YamlValue` — surfacing it
//     is the no-engine honesty, never a non-terminating walk or a
//     fabricated truncation). The resolution itself is the deferred
//     parse body's job (🟡 below); the data model needs no alias variant.
//   - TAG RESOLUTION (YAML 1.2.2 §10.3 core schema): which scalar
//     variant a plain node resolves to (null/bool/int/float/str) is the
//     core-schema tag-resolution rule, applied in the deferred parser;
//     the data model carries the RESOLVED variant, not the raw node.
//
// `Bool`/`String` are kernel-ambient (v2 seed; STRUCTURE.md "Kernel-
// ambient types"); `List<T>`/`Map<K,V>` are kernel container types
// (node.dag uses `List` unimported). That relaxes only the import edge —
// this file remains YAML's sole modeling authority (the std/logic.dag
// kernel-ambient discipline). No std/ import is needed: the data model
// uses only kernel-ambient carriers + self-recursion; the deferred
// carrier seam is named in prose, not structurally referenced (an
// unused import would itself fail 0-diagnostics).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// YamlValue — the YAML 1.2.2 value: the closed, recursive set of the
// seven YAML core-schema value kinds. Anchor: YAML 1.2.2 §10.3 (core
// schema) — https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#103-core-schema — over the
// §2.1 representation graph (https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#21-collections).
//
//   - YamlNull        — the `null` core-schema value (YAML 1.2.2 §10.3.2
//                        `tag:yaml.org,2002:null`).
//   - YamlBool        — `true`/`false` (§10.3.2 `…:bool`); the payload is
//                        the kernel `Bool` (one authority for the truth
//                        value — std/logic.dag).
//   - YamlInt         — §10.3.2 `…:int`. Carries the VERBATIM source
//                        scalar (`lexeme`), NOT a numeric value — the
//                        json.dag `JsonNumber` lexeme-preserving decision
//                        applied to YAML's int tag: YAML 1.2.2 separates
//                        tag RESOLUTION (which decides this is the `int`
//                        variant) from numeric INTERPRETATION; the
//                        faithful integer carrier (std/integer.dag) is
//                        scaffold, so collapsing now would fabricate a
//                        range/precision decision (no-fabrication) and
//                        lose round-trip (B-6) exactness. Numeric
//                        interpretation is a deferred read (🟡 below).
//   - YamlFloat       — §10.3.2 `…:float` (incl. `.inf`/`.nan`). Carries
//                        the verbatim `lexeme` for the same reason as
//                        `YamlInt`; faithful carrier std/float.dag is
//                        scaffold (IEEE-754 collapse = fabrication +
//                        breaks B-6). Numeric interpretation deferred.
//   - YamlString      — §10.3.2 `…:str`. The payload is the kernel
//                        `String` (escape / block-scalar / quoting
//                        decoding is the deferred parse body's concern,
//                        not a data-model variant).
//   - YamlSequence    — YAML 1.2.2 §2.1 sequence. An ordered series of
//                        YamlValue; carrier is the kernel
//                        `List<YamlValue>` (order is the list order —
//                        the single authority, canonical/deterministic
//                        per CULTURE §8).
//   - YamlMapping     — YAML 1.2.2 §2.1 mapping. An unordered set of
//                        key/value pairs; YAML permits NON-STRING keys
//                        (§2.2), so the carrier is the kernel
//                        `Map<YamlValue, YamlValue>` (per the immutable
//                        scaffold header — key AND value are `YamlValue`).
//                        KEY-UNIQUENESS — HONEST SCOPE (do NOT over-
//                        claim): `Map` structurally dedups by `YamlValue`
//                        STRUCTURAL equality (lexeme-sensitive). But
//                        YAML 1.2.2 §3.2.1.3 (node comparison) defines
//                        mapping-key uniqueness by TAG + CANONICAL
//                        content, NOT by surface lexeme — so two
//                        lexeme-DISTINCT but canonically-EQUAL scalar
//                        keys under the same tag (e.g. `0x10` and `16`,
//                        both `!!int`; `1.0` and `1.0e0`, both
//                        `!!float`) are §3.2.1.3 DUPLICATES yet are
//                        DISTINCT `YamlValue`s here (the lexeme-
//                        preserving decision: `YamlInt`/`YamlFloat`
//                        carry the verbatim lexeme because the numeric/
//                        tag CANONICALIZATION rides the deferred
//                        integer/float substrate). Therefore `Map` does
//                        NOT make §3.2.1.3-duplicate keys structurally
//                        unrepresentable; it only forecloses lexeme-
//                        IDENTICAL duplicates. Full §3.2.1.3 key
//                        uniqueness (canonicalize each key by its
//                        resolved tag, reject canonically-equal
//                        duplicates as a fail-closed `Rejected`) is a
//                        DEFERRED-PARSER invariant — 🟡 (3) below — not
//                        a structural carrier guarantee; honestly
//                        scoped here per the std/collection.dag #3169
//                        "do not over-claim what the carrier provides"
//                        precedent (no silent last-wins = no-engine;
//                        the parser is the §3.2.1.3 authority until the
//                        canonicalization substrate lands). String-keyed
//                        formats (json.dag `Map<String,..>`, toml.dag
//                        `Map<String,..>`) do NOT have this gap — their
//                        keys are byte-strings, so Map dedup IS the
//                        spec's key equality; this is YAML-specific
//                        (non-string keys + lexeme-preserved scalars),
//                        not a canonical-seam defect.
//
// Recursion lives in the `elements` / `entries` children (node.dag A1:
// the one recursive type is `Node`; this recursive `Disj` is a finite
// Node tree whose recursive occurrence is the name-reference `YamlValue`
// resolved through the namespace — never a self-referential type slot,
// the std/nat.dag `Succ { prev: Nat }` shape; `YamlMapping` recurses in
// BOTH key and value position, both `YamlValue`). REGULAR recursion
// (the recursive occurrence is `YamlValue` itself) ⇒ unconditionally
// admissible per A1; structural descent on a proper sub-YamlValue is the
// A2 IMPLICIT termination case, total by construction, no ranking
// measure. (A finite `YamlValue` is alias-RESOLVED — see Modeling notes;
// an alias cycle never produces a value, it produces a fail-closed
// `Rejected`, so the recursive type stays well-founded.)
//
// CLOSED. The seven are YAML 1.2.2 §10.3's exhaustive core-schema value
// space — no `Other` / open variant. An eighth kind would be an edit to
// this declaration routed to ratification (it would mean the YAML core
// schema changed), not a silent escape.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the value kind is consumed UNIFORMLY by
//      every YAML consumer (the deferred parse builds it, emit walks it,
//      any pipeline ingest dispatches on it). It is the shared
//      classifier of the whole YAML value space, not a per-consumer
//      scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the seven carry structurally DISTINCT
//      payloads (YamlNull nullary; a `Bool`; an int `lexeme` String; a
//      float `lexeme` String; a `String`; a `List<YamlValue>`; a
//      `Map<YamlValue, YamlValue>`). Collapsing to a flat `{ kind,
//      bool?, int?, float?, string?, seq?, map? }` record makes illegal
//      mixed states representable (a YamlNull carrying sequence
//      elements; a YamlBool with mapping entries) — the inverse of
//      INVARIANTS P2 (illegal states unrepresentable).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: a YAML value is YAML
//      1.2.2 §10.3's core-schema value space; it is heterogeneous
//      external data, not the carrier of a richer std/algebra.dag
//      structure (no Monoid/Ring/Lattice it inhabits). The Sequence/
//      Mapping children ground in their OWN carriers (List = FreeMonoid,
//      Map) owned by std/collection.dag — not a richer single source
//      YamlValue decomposes into; pointing the variants at an algebra
//      is circular.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a YAML value is EXACTLY ONE of the seven
//      kinds (YAML 1.2.2 §10.3 core-schema tag resolution yields one
//      tag); mutually-exclusive alternatives, never simultaneous
//      coordinates. No value is "both an int and a sequence".
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the seven variants are
//      structurally heterogeneous (nullary; `Bool`; int `lexeme`;
//      float `lexeme`; `String`; `List<YamlValue>`; `Map<YamlValue,
//      YamlValue>`) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a
//      separately-declared enumerable set (Practice 7). They are the
//      irreducible constructors of the YAML 1.2.2 core-schema value
//      space, not a mechanical copy of an enumerable set.
// Terminal: the irreducible closed YAML 1.2.2 §10.3 core-schema value
// space; a recursive Disj whose Sequence/Mapping children recurse
// through kernel `List`/`Map` of `YamlValue` (A1 recursion-in-children,
// name-reference through the namespace — the std/nat.dag `Nat` shape).
type YamlValue
  = YamlNull
  | YamlBool { value: Bool }
  | YamlInt { lexeme: String }
  | YamlFloat { lexeme: String }
  | YamlString { value: String }
  | YamlSequence { elements: List<YamlValue> }
  | YamlMapping { entries: Map<YamlValue, YamlValue> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deferred — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡). The operations ride substrate that
// is scaffold today. Per the operator-ratified declarative-now /
// operations-deferred-with-owner discipline (verified on std/collection.
// dag #3169 + canonical json.dag #3184) these are NOT authored here —
// not improvised bodies, not bodiless `fn` (the frozen v2 seed rejects
// bodiless `fn`, DECISIONS PARSE-1). Each carries the three bridge
// properties (scaffold doc / bounds on use / dissolution trigger).
//
// (1) `yaml_parse` / `yaml_emit` — the parse and emit operation bodies.
//   A YAML parser is a Char-STREAM structural walk over the YAML 1.2.2
//   §5–§9 productions and emit builds a String from structure; both
//   require std/text.dag's `Char` + a `String` ↔ sequence-of-`Char`
//   decomposition — std/text.dag is T-3 SCAFFOLD. RATIFIED parse
//   strategy (mirrors json.dag): RECURSIVE-DESCENT single self-recursive
//   structural walk over the input Char-sequence spine, with the YAML
//   representation-graph phases (compose nodes → resolve `&anchor`/
//   `*alias` against the anchor table → apply §10.3.2 tag resolution →
//   produce the alias-free, tag-resolved `YamlValue`); termination is
//   the A2 IMPLICIT case (descent on a proper sub-sequence), and an
//   alias CYCLE is a fail-closed `Rejected` (never a non-terminating
//   walk — the no-engine honesty).
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `yaml_parse`/`yaml_emit` are
//         intentionally absent from yaml.dag today.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer reads `yaml_parse`/`yaml_emit`
//         from yaml.dag in this PR. The carrier seam is fixed: when
//         authored, `yaml_parse : String -> Outcome<YamlValue>` and
//         `yaml_emit : YamlValue -> String`, `Outcome<T>` the ratified
//         D1 carrier — fail-closed (every non-conforming input, every
//         unresolvable/cyclic alias, is `Rejected { diagnostic }`,
//         never a partial/defaulted value). The SAME seam as the other
//         five T-4.6 formats.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/text.dag (T-3) lands its
//         `Char` + `String` ↔ `List<Char>` decomposition, a follow-up
//         PR adds `yaml_parse`/`yaml_emit` (with anchor/alias + tag
//         resolution) as single self-recursive structural walks
//         returning `Outcome<YamlValue>`; this note dissolves. The T-4
//         directive's "operations deferred-with-named-owner" + TASKS.md
//         T-4.6 record the same dependency.
//
// (2) Numeric interpretation of `YamlInt.lexeme` / `YamlFloat.lexeme` —
//   YAML 1.2.2 §10.3.2 tag resolution decides the int-vs-float VARIANT
//   (carried above); the numeric VALUE is the implementation's concern
//   and the faithful carriers are scaffold: the integer tower
//   (std/integer.dag) and IEEE-754 `Float` (std/float.dag), both T-3.
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; `YamlInt`/`YamlFloat` carry the
//         lexeme, not a numeric value; no interpretation is claimed.
//     (b) bounds on use — a consumer needing the numeric value waits on
//         the interpretation read. The lexeme is round-trip-exact
//         (B-6): emit reproduces the source scalar with zero precision
//         loss, which a premature numeric collapse could not.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/integer.dag + std/float.dag
//         land, a follow-up adds `yaml_int_value`/`yaml_float_value :
//         … -> Outcome<…>`; this note dissolves.
//
// (3) YAML 1.2.2 §3.2.1.3 canonical mapping-key uniqueness — the
//   honest scope of the `YamlMapping` key dedup (see the YamlMapping
//   bullet above). §3.2.1.3 (node comparison,
//   https://yaml.org/spec/1.2.2/#3213-node-comparison) defines mapping
//   keys as duplicate iff equal by TAG + CANONICAL content. The
//   `Map<YamlValue, YamlValue>` carrier only forecloses lexeme-
//   IDENTICAL duplicate keys; canonically-equal-but-lexeme-distinct
//   scalar keys (e.g. `0x10`/`16` `!!int`; `1.0`/`1.0e0` `!!float`;
//   tag-distinct same-content) remain representable because
//   canonicalization needs the resolved tag + numeric value that is
//   deferred (item (2)). This is surfaced honestly, NOT over-claimed
//   as a structural guarantee (the std/collection.dag #3169
//   over-claim-correction precedent).
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note + the YamlMapping bullet; the
//         carrier's structural dedup is lexeme-scoped, explicitly not
//         the full §3.2.1.3 relation.
//     (b) bounds on use — no consumer may assume a `YamlMapping` is
//         §3.2.1.3-dup-free from the type alone. The deferred parser
//         is the §3.2.1.3 authority: it resolves each key's tag,
//         canonicalizes, and rejects canonically-equal duplicates as a
//         fail-closed `Rejected` (no silent last-wins = no-engine) —
//         the same fail-closed parse-construction discipline as
//         alias-cycle and tag resolution.
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when the tag-resolution + numeric/
//         scalar canonicalization substrate lands (rides std/text.dag
//         §10.3.2 tag resolution + std/integer.dag/std/float.dag
//         canonical forms — items (1)/(2)), a follow-up makes key
//         canonicalization a structural normalization so §3.2.1.3
//         uniqueness becomes carrier-enforced; this note dissolves.
//         A structural key-canonicalization that would need a new
//         substrate concept is a STOP, not a worker workaround.

